1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,337
This is every commit on our two triage
pull requests, on one clock:

2
00:00:04,337 --> 00:00:07,509
Boston time,
from Tuesday noon to early Thursday.

3
00:00:07,829 --> 00:00:11,242
109 commits, every one made by an agent:

4
00:00:11,242 --> 00:00:15,765
88 changes,
and 21 merges of one branch into another.

5
00:00:16,085 --> 00:00:21,120
GitHub shows forty of them under your name,
because the agent used your git identity.

6
00:00:21,367 --> 00:00:22,412
Two questions.

7
00:00:22,732 --> 00:00:24,439
Why are most of them fixes?

8
00:00:24,759 --> 00:00:30,668
53 of the 88 name a review in their message:
Bugbot, Codex, or a review agent.

9
00:00:30,988 --> 00:00:34,593
And no person has reviewed them yet,
so who decided anything?

10
00:00:35,933 --> 00:00:37,277
First, the machinery.

11
00:00:37,597 --> 00:00:40,652
Each job starts as an issue,
gets its own branch,

12
00:00:40,652 --> 00:00:44,018
and opens a draft pull request
after the first commit.

13
00:00:44,338 --> 00:00:47,850
Pushes wake two review bots
from different companies:

14
00:00:47,850 --> 00:00:49,838
Cursor's Bugbot on every push,

15
00:00:49,838 --> 00:00:54,344
and OpenAI's Codex once the pull request
is marked ready for review.

16
00:00:54,664 --> 00:00:56,968
They may comment, but never change code.

17
00:00:57,216 --> 00:00:58,468
When an agent stops,

18
00:00:58,468 --> 00:01:01,285
it writes a handoff note
on the pull request,

19
00:01:01,285 --> 00:01:03,936
marked unattended when nobody was watching.

20
00:01:04,256 --> 00:01:06,578
Big choices become decision records,

21
00:01:06,578 --> 00:01:09,803
marked proposed
until a person marks them adopted.

22
00:01:10,123 --> 00:01:14,326
The team's rule: chat is temporary,
the repository is permanent.

23
00:01:14,566 --> 00:01:19,098
Two agent sessions made the commits:
the first until just after midnight,

24
00:01:19,098 --> 00:01:24,251
the second from one fifty-one on Wednesday
morning to a quarter to one on Thursday.

25
00:01:24,571 --> 00:01:27,858
The chat page was stacked
on top of the triage brain,

26
00:01:27,858 --> 00:01:30,524
and pulled in the brain's changes 13 times.

27
00:01:32,167 --> 00:01:35,362
Tuesday, at twelve twenty-two,
after a night of planning,

28
00:01:35,362 --> 00:01:37,436
the first session started committing.

29
00:01:37,756 --> 00:01:42,646
Within forty minutes it had a skeleton:
fact cards from 15 sources,

30
00:01:42,646 --> 00:01:47,975
a rules engine that decided urgency,
eleven test cases and an evaluation.

31
00:01:48,217 --> 00:01:49,710
Then it checked its own facts.

32
00:01:50,030 --> 00:01:54,706
Four reviewer agents that could not talk
to each other read every card

33
00:01:54,706 --> 00:01:57,646
against its source and reported 75 problems.

34
00:01:57,966 --> 00:02:02,041
Twenty-three minutes
after the first 74 cards, there were 97.

35
00:02:02,361 --> 00:02:06,233
One had said out-of-network urgent care
costs you 20 percent;

36
00:02:06,233 --> 00:02:10,233
the source said you pay
a five-hundred-dollar deductible first.

37
00:02:11,567 --> 00:02:14,475
At nine twenty-one
that evening, Boston time,

38
00:02:14,475 --> 00:02:18,095
Wednesday morning in Beijing,
a commit changed the plan.

39
00:02:18,415 --> 00:02:22,098
The rules engine matched words,
and word lists both missed things

40
00:02:22,098 --> 00:02:25,668
and flagged the wrong things;
every patch broke something else.

41
00:02:25,917 --> 00:02:31,569
The record says you decided: a demo first,
evaluation later; the model judges urgency

42
00:02:31,569 --> 00:02:32,647
and where to go,

43
00:02:32,647 --> 00:02:36,690
and the checker on its output
becomes the only safety layer.

44
00:02:37,010 --> 00:02:38,029
The rules engine

45
00:02:38,029 --> 00:02:41,469
and the comparison experiments stay
in the repository,

46
00:02:41,469 --> 00:02:42,600
no longer pursued.

47
00:02:42,834 --> 00:02:46,051
But notice:
the checker is also made of word rules,

48
00:02:46,051 --> 00:02:48,637
applied to sentences instead of symptoms.

49
00:02:48,957 --> 00:02:50,748
What would make it less of a whack-a-mole?

50
00:02:54,983 --> 00:02:56,092
Then came the loop.

51
00:02:56,412 --> 00:02:59,191
At ten twenty-three that night, Boston time,

52
00:02:59,191 --> 00:03:02,854
the agent marked the brain's pull request
ready for review

53
00:03:02,854 --> 00:03:07,527
and asked two teammates to look,
and both bots began reviewing every push.

54
00:03:07,847 --> 00:03:11,218
Push, the bots review, the agent fixes,
push again.

55
00:03:11,538 --> 00:03:13,165
Between ten twenty-seven

56
00:03:13,165 --> 00:03:17,231
and just after midnight
the first session pushed 19 commits,

57
00:03:17,231 --> 00:03:19,196
about one every five minutes.

58
00:03:19,516 --> 00:03:23,191
On that pull request
the bots wrote 72 comments,

59
00:03:23,191 --> 00:03:24,722
and 64 led to fixes.

60
00:03:24,966 --> 00:03:29,103
By half past midnight,
you and the agent had agreed on one change:

61
00:03:29,103 --> 00:03:31,046
batch the fixes before pushing.

62
00:03:32,383 --> 00:03:35,329
On Wednesday the second session
built the chat page

63
00:03:35,329 --> 00:03:38,506
from episode three and kept
fixing the brain beside it.

64
00:03:38,826 --> 00:03:42,947
In the afternoon a final review
looked from five angles at once

65
00:03:42,947 --> 00:03:44,778
and found about 30 problems.

66
00:03:45,016 --> 00:03:49,268
Six fix agents took them one after another,
each on its own branch,

67
00:03:49,268 --> 00:03:52,504
and each first reproduced its bug
before fixing it.

68
00:03:52,750 --> 00:03:54,883
Why reproduce a bug before fixing it?

69
00:03:59,750 --> 00:04:02,714
Because a reproduction proves
the bug is real,

70
00:04:02,714 --> 00:04:06,515
and gives a check
that has to flip from failing to passing;

71
00:04:06,515 --> 00:04:09,542
a fix for a bug nobody
saw fail proves nothing.

72
00:04:09,862 --> 00:04:13,928
Merging the branches back took about an hour
and had collisions;

73
00:04:13,928 --> 00:04:18,438
in one, a branch added a field
that another branch's new test rejected.

74
00:04:18,758 --> 00:04:21,723
A second review of the merged result
found eight more.

75
00:04:21,966 --> 00:04:26,285
Along the way the session hit
its five-hour usage limit twice,

76
00:04:26,285 --> 00:04:27,342
the second time

77
00:04:27,342 --> 00:04:32,206
because the six fix agents ran
in a row at about 800,000 tokens each,

78
00:04:32,206 --> 00:04:34,574
and the container restarted twice.

79
00:04:34,894 --> 00:04:38,094
The agents' rule afterwards:
at most three at a time.

80
00:04:39,433 --> 00:04:40,628
Back to the checker.

81
00:04:40,948 --> 00:04:43,017
Most of the bots' comments were about it.

82
00:04:43,337 --> 00:04:48,249
After the turn it grew
from 459 lines to 1,326,

83
00:04:48,249 --> 00:04:52,638
and 27 of the 45 commits since touched it.

84
00:04:52,958 --> 00:04:54,601
Each fix moved the problem.

85
00:04:54,921 --> 00:04:58,285
On Wednesday a parent summary
saying don't delay help

86
00:04:58,285 --> 00:05:01,840
because of insurance was flagged
as putting money first,

87
00:05:01,840 --> 00:05:06,220
which cost a needless rewrite;
that evening, narrowing a similar rule

88
00:05:06,220 --> 00:05:10,345
let a real money-first line slip through,
until Bugbot caught it.

89
00:05:10,583 --> 00:05:13,954
So on the second night the agents
stopped waiting for the bots.

90
00:05:14,274 --> 00:05:18,388
Two rules took the beating:
never claim a clinic is open right now

91
00:05:18,388 --> 00:05:20,220
unless its hours are checked,

92
00:05:20,220 --> 00:05:23,063
and keep cost out
of the way of an emergency.

93
00:05:23,383 --> 00:05:26,830
Before pushing,
the agents attacked those two rules:

94
00:05:26,830 --> 00:05:30,078
they wrote about 3,000 ways
to say a risky thing,

95
00:05:30,078 --> 00:05:32,663
or its harmless twin, and ran them all.

96
00:05:32,983 --> 00:05:38,039
The first round found 142 misses
and 230 false alarms.

97
00:05:38,283 --> 00:05:39,926
The bots would have found these too.

98
00:05:40,246 --> 00:05:42,272
Why attack the checker yourself first?

99
00:05:45,283 --> 00:05:47,976
Because the bots
report one finding at a time,

100
00:05:47,976 --> 00:05:50,318
and every fix waits for the next review.

101
00:05:50,638 --> 00:05:53,944
One probing run found 372 at once.

102
00:05:54,264 --> 00:05:58,264
Across both rounds,
1,060 phrasings it got wrong were fixed

103
00:05:58,264 --> 00:06:01,450
and kept as tests that run
with the unit tests;

104
00:06:01,450 --> 00:06:04,974
about twenty harder ones
are listed as known misses.

105
00:06:05,294 --> 00:06:09,551
In the second round, the first fix
even created 20 new false alarms;

106
00:06:09,551 --> 00:06:12,931
this time they were caught before the push,
not after.

107
00:06:14,267 --> 00:06:15,355
What did it cost?

108
00:06:15,675 --> 00:06:19,351
The model calls the product made
are on the record:

109
00:06:19,351 --> 00:06:24,686
about 4 cents for one real answer,
about 77 cents for the whole first day,

110
00:06:24,686 --> 00:06:28,219
27 cents on the last night
against a 50-cent cap.

111
00:06:28,539 --> 00:06:32,583
The agents' own work
is priced nowhere in the repository;

112
00:06:32,583 --> 00:06:36,485
the only number is tokens:
about 800,000 per fix agent,

113
00:06:36,485 --> 00:06:41,168
close to five million for the six,
and none recorded for the rest.

114
00:06:42,917 --> 00:06:44,154
So who decided?

115
00:06:44,474 --> 00:06:47,827
The record, written by the agents,
names your calls,

116
00:06:47,827 --> 00:06:52,406
among them the model and its effort,
the turn to the model's judgement,

117
00:06:52,406 --> 00:06:56,340
batching the fixes,
copying Claude's page, and the stop rule:

118
00:06:56,340 --> 00:06:57,581
stop at 99 percent,

119
00:06:57,581 --> 00:07:01,306
finish by Thursday at seven in the evening,
Beijing time.

120
00:07:01,626 --> 00:07:05,293
The code kept it: the last push
came more than six hours early,

121
00:07:05,293 --> 00:07:09,136
and four later bot findings
were written down for the next person.

122
00:07:09,384 --> 00:07:14,235
Many smaller choices the agents made alone,
and wrote down as proposals,

123
00:07:14,235 --> 00:07:17,469
marked unattended
and not confirmed by a person.

124
00:07:17,789 --> 00:07:21,234
One handoff even lists,
among the agent's own steps,

125
00:07:21,234 --> 00:07:24,547
a click the team's workflow
reserves for a person:

126
00:07:24,547 --> 00:07:27,197
marking a pull request ready for review.

127
00:07:27,517 --> 00:07:29,747
The notes are honest about slips too:

128
00:07:29,747 --> 00:07:31,394
one admits an earlier claim

129
00:07:31,394 --> 00:07:34,749
that all Bugbot comments were fixed,
when two were not.

130
00:07:34,984 --> 00:07:36,418
Which raises a question:

131
00:07:36,418 --> 00:07:40,722
with no person having reviewed it yet,
what makes this code trustworthy?

132
00:07:44,867 --> 00:07:46,296
No single check does.

133
00:07:46,616 --> 00:07:51,617
It is layers: hundreds of tests,
one of which replays the 1,060 probes,

134
00:07:51,617 --> 00:07:55,685
two review bots,
and decision records that stay proposals

135
00:07:55,685 --> 00:07:57,112
until you sign them.

136
00:07:57,432 --> 00:08:00,198
Decision four itself is still a proposal,

137
00:08:00,198 --> 00:08:03,572
and before any real user it asks
for an evaluation

138
00:08:03,572 --> 00:08:06,542
and a look from the team's medical reviewer.

139
00:08:06,862 --> 00:08:10,706
Twelve more questions wait for you,
from decision six's addendum

140
00:08:10,706 --> 00:08:14,371
to whether a parent should get a dial link
for a crisis line.

141
00:08:19,117 --> 00:08:22,787
So, two days:
a skeleton and a fact check in an afternoon;

142
00:08:22,787 --> 00:08:24,622
your turn at nine twenty-one;

143
00:08:24,622 --> 00:08:28,481
a night of whack-a-mole with the bots;
a queue of fix agents;

144
00:08:28,481 --> 00:08:32,405
a second night of attacking the checker
before the bots could;

145
00:08:32,405 --> 00:08:34,050
and a stop rule that held.

146
00:08:34,284 --> 00:08:35,393
Three questions.

147
00:08:35,713 --> 00:08:38,444
Why did so many of the brain's commits
touch the checker?

148
00:08:38,764 --> 00:08:40,897
Why reproduce a bug before fixing it?

149
00:08:41,217 --> 00:08:42,860
And what is still waiting for you?

150
00:08:48,100 --> 00:08:50,468
Two more pull requests arrived on Friday.

151
00:08:50,788 --> 00:08:53,583
Next:
how an agent proves its change is right.

